WEBVTT - Supply Chain Infiltration: The Counterfeit Vendor (English)

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The most dangerous scam is one that arrives inside an ongoing conversation with someone you trust.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:36.000
For two weeks, a manufacturing plant had been emailing with their steel supplier. On day fourteen, an invoice arrived with new bank details.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Even though the supplier's account was hacked, if the victim hit 'Reply', the real supplier would answer and expose the scam.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:15.000
So the attacker set a hidden trapdoor called a 'Reply-To' divergence. TraceXMail spotted it immediately.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:28.000
While the letter showed the supplier's name, any reply would secretly be diverted to a scammer's private inbox.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:50.000
TraceXMail examined the attached invoice inside a safe quarantine container. It revealed altered bank account numbers.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:12.000
TraceXMail built a clear timeline showing the exact half-hour window when the hacker broke into the email and altered the bill.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The purchasing manager called the supplier directly to alert them. The payment was held, saving $680,000.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:48.000
With TraceXMail’s easy report, the supplier was able to clean up their compromised account and protect their partnership.
